Two independent, never-stale log samples both showed no anomalies, so healthy is now about 97% likely. Submitting healthy has an expected loss of about 1.9 (60 × 0.032). Every remaining tool has a negative value, so calling one would cost more than the loss it could remove. Escalating would cost 8. I'm submitting healthy.

{"action": "submit", "verdict": "healthy"}

Posterior after `no_errors` from the log scan: verified ≈ 0.90, incomplete ≈ 0.08, corrupt ≈ 0.02. Submitting "verified" now would cost about 60 × 0.10 = 6 in expected loss, and escalating costs 8, so I need more evidence.

`backup_status` costs 1. It reports "verified" about 95% of the time when the backup really is verified, and only about 3% of the time when it isn't. A "verified" answer would therefore push the posterior above 0.99 for roughly 1.2 expected total cost, which is much cheaper than `restore_test` at 4.

{"action": "call", "tool": "backup_status"}

After one all_noerror reading, my posterior is about 0.95 resolving, 0.04 intermittent and 0.01 failing. Submitting "resolving" now has an expected loss of about 3.0, mostly from the 60-point penalty if it is actually intermittent or failing.

A second query_log_sample call would cost 1 and lower the expected loss only a little, because a conflicting reading would leave me close to escalating anyway. dns_status is a better buy. A "resolving" reading from it is weaker evidence, with a likelihood ratio of about 3.2. But the posterior is already high, so the main value is catching a non-resolving state. Its non-resolving readings are informative, since a stale replica almost never emits them. I estimate an expected total of about 2.6 including the call cost, against 3.0 for submitting now.

{"action": "call", "tool": "dns_status"}

The fourth query-log reading (all_noerror) settles it. Across four reads the log shows three all_noerror and one some_servfail. Combined with the prior and the odd dns_status "failing" reading, my posterior is about 0.99 resolving, 0.006 intermittent and 0.006 failing.

Submitting "resolving" has an expected loss of about 60 × 0.012 ≈ 0.7. One more query_log call would cost 1, which is more than it could save, and escalating costs 8. dig_probe is unaffordable with 1 unit left. So I'm submitting now.

{"action": "submit", "verdict": "resolving"}

